        ":question/id": "5e9cc9e7-1d62-51d1-8fa2-dd51fa7daa28",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-130257",
        ":question/problem": "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ at the point $(2,1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{\u2032}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{\u2032}(a,b)=\u2207f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2016v\u2016 & =\\sqrt{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}+{1}^{2}}=2 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $\u2207f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(xy)\\text{\u00a0}i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(xy)\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =y\\,i+x\\,j \\\\ \u2207\\,f(2,1) & =i+2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{\u2032}(2,1) & =\u2207\\,f(2,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1(\\sqrt{3})+2(1)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
