        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-130260",
        ":question/problem": "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})$ at the point $(0,-1)$ in the direction of the vector $v=3\\,i+4\\,j.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{\u2032}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{\u2032}(a,b)=\u2207f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the unit vector $u$ in the direction of $v:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2016v\u2016 & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}=5 \\\\ u & =\\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the gradient $\u2207f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $(0,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207\\,f(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{\u00a0}i+\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\operatorname{ln}\u2061({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}))\\text{\u00a0}j \\\\  & =\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}j \\\\ \u2207\\,f(0,-1) & =\\frac{2(0)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2(-1)}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{\u2032}(0,-1) & =\u2207\\,f(0,-1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =-2\\,j\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}(3\\,i+4\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2(4)}{5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
