        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/id": "4c3374df-275b-5fb9-8864-40efeec8b5b4",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-36306",
        ":question/problem": "Given the function  $g(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)^{2}$, find its directional derivative at the\npoint $A(-1,1)$ in the direction that's towards the point $B(3,4).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{\u2032}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{\u2032}(a,b)=\u2207f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\u27e83,4\u27e9-\u27e8-1,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e84,3\u27e9 \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{\u2016\\overset{\\to }{AB}\u2016} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{4}^{2}}}\u27e84,3\u27e9 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\u27e84,3\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\u2207g(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(-1,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207g(x,y) & =\u27e8\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({x}^{2}+y)^{2},\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}({x}^{2}+y)^{2}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e84x({x}^{2}+y),\\,2({x}^{2}+y)\u27e9 \\\\ \u2207g(-1,1) & =\u27e84\\cdot (-1)\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1),2\\cdot ((-1)^{2}+1)\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-8,4\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}_{u}^{\u2032}(-1,1) & =\u2207g(-1,1)\\cdot u \\\\  & =\u27e8-8,4\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}\u27e9 \\\\  & =-8\\cdot \\frac{4}{5}+4\\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
