        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/id": "5fc2a3e0-1de4-50ca-b644-c592b3edb577",
        ":question/math-academy-id": "q-130268",
        ":question/problem": "Find the directional derivative of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ at the point $A(4,3)$ in the direction towards the point $B(1,1).$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "The directional derivative ${f}_{u}^{\u2032}(a,b)$ of a function $f(x,y)$ at the point $(a,b)$ in the direction of the vector $v$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{u}^{\u2032}(a,b)=\u2207f(a,b)\\cdot u\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is a unit vector in the direction of $v.$\n\nFirst, we find the direction vector and the corresponding unit vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\u27e81,1\u27e9-\u27e84,3\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-3,-2\u27e9 \\\\ u & =\\frac{\\overset{\\to }{AB}}{\u2016\\overset{\\to }{AB}\u2016} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}}\u27e8-3,-2\u27e9 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{13}}\u27e8-3,-2\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the gradient $\u2207f(x,y)$ and evaluate it at the point $A(4,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\u2207f(x,y) & =\u27e8\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}),\\,\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\u27e9 \\\\ \u2207f(4,3) & =\u27e8\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}},\\,\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{3}^{2}}}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the directional derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{u}^{\u2032}(4,3) & =\u2207f(4,3)\\cdot u \\\\  & =\u27e8\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{3}{5}\u27e9\\cdot \u27e8-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}},-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\u27e9 \\\\  & =\\frac{4(-3)+3(-2)}{5\\sqrt{13}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{18}{5\\sqrt{13}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"
      },
